\n* Risk of brain or blood-vessel injury.\r\n* Intracranial bleeding.\r\n* Stroke or neurological deficit.\r\n* Seizures.\r\n* Infection.\r\n* CSF leak.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Aneurysm Coiling\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Endovascular coiling is a minimally invasive procedure in which detachable coils are introduced through a catheter into the aneurysm, causing thrombosis and preventing blood flow into the aneurysm. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. Local or general anesthesia is administered.\r\n2. A catheter is introduced through an artery, commonly through the groin or wrist.\r\n3. The catheter is guided through the arterial system to the cerebral circulation.\r\n4. The aneurysm is identified using angiography.\r\n5. A microcatheter is passed into the aneurysm.\r\n6. Soft platinum coils are deployed inside the aneurysm.\r\n7. Coils promote clot formation within the aneurysm.\r\n8. Blood flow into the aneurysm is reduced or stopped.\r\n9. The catheter is removed.\r\n10. The access site is closed and monitored. \r\n\r\nAdvantages\r\n\r\n* Minimally invasive.\r\n* No craniotomy is required.\r\n* Generally shorter recovery than surgical clipping.\r\n* Particularly useful when open surgery carries greater risk.\r\n* Endovascular treatment can be advantageous in selected patients with ruptured aneurysms. \r\n\r\nDisadvantages / Complications\r\n\r\n* Aneurysm may recanalize or recur.\r\n* May require repeat treatment and follow-up imaging.\r\n* Thromboembolic complications.\r\n* Vessel injury.\r\n* Intracranial hemorrhage.\r\n* Stroke or neurological deficit.\r\n* Contrast-related complications.\r\n* Some wide-neck aneurysms may require balloon- or stent-assisted techniques. 4. Factors Affecting Choice of Treatment\r\n\r\nThe decision between clipping and coiling depends on:\r\n\r\n* Aneurysm size.\r\n* Aneurysm location.\r\n* Shape and neck width.\r\n* Ruptured or unruptured status.\r\n* Patient’s age.\r\n* General medical condition.\r\n* Presence of brain swelling or vasospasm.\r\n* Accessibility of the aneurysm.\r\n* Experience of the treating neurosurgical/endovascular team. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Physiotherapy Implications\r\n\r\n* Assess:\r\n    * Level of consciousness.\r\n    * Motor power.\r\n    * Sensory function.\r\n    * Muscle tone.\r\n    * Balance and coordination.\r\n    * Respiratory status.\r\n    * Functional mobility.\r\n* Maintain appropriate positioning.\r\n* Provide respiratory physiotherapy when clinically indicated.\r\n* Perform ROM exercises to prevent stiffness and contractures.\r\n* Begin early and progressive mobilization when medically stable.\r\n* Provide balance, gait and functional training.\r\n* Monitor neurological status during treatment.\r\n* Educate patient and family regarding safe mobility and rehabilitation./////////////////////Postoperative Complications\r\n\r\nShock, DVT, Wound Infection, Atelectasis, Pneumonia, UTI and Pressure Sores\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nPostoperative complications are unwanted conditions that develop after a surgical procedure and may delay recovery, impair normal body function, increase hospital stay, or threaten life.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Shock\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Shock is a state of inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery resulting in cellular dysfunction and organ failure.\r\n\r\nCauses after surgery\r\n\r\n* Hemorrhage.\r\n* Severe fluid loss.\r\n* Sepsis.\r\n* Cardiac dysfunction.\r\n* Anaphylaxis.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Hypotension.\r\n* Tachycardia.\r\n* Cold, clammy skin.\r\n* Rapid breathing.\r\n* Reduced urine output.\r\n* Confusion or reduced consciousness.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Do not mobilize an unstable patient.\r\n* Monitor BP, HR, respiratory rate and SpO₂.\r\n* Position appropriately according to the medical condition.\r\n* Once stabilized, gradually initiate mobility and rehabilitation.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* DVT is the formation of a blood clot within a deep vein, most commonly in the lower limb.\r\n\r\nRisk factors\r\n\r\n* Prolonged immobilization.\r\n* Major surgery.\r\n* Reduced mobility.\r\n* Dehydration.\r\n* Previous DVT.\r\n* Cancer.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Unilateral leg swelling.\r\n* Calf pain or tenderness.\r\n* Warmth.\r\n* Redness.\r\n* Increased calf circumference.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Encourage early mobilization when medically appropriate.\r\n* Ankle-pump and active limb exercises.\r\n* Avoid vigorous massage of a suspected DVT.\r\n* If DVT is suspected, stop treatment and seek medical assessment.\r\n* Anticoagulation and mechanical prophylaxis may be prescribed according to risk.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Wound Infection\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Wound infection is infection of a surgical incision or wound by microorganisms, causing local and/or systemic inflammatory response.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Increased pain.\r\n* Redness.\r\n* Swelling.\r\n* Warmth.\r\n* Purulent discharge.\r\n* Fever.\r\n* Delayed wound healing.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Inspect the wound before treatment.\r\n* Maintain strict hand hygiene and infection-control precautions.\r\n* Avoid direct pressure or vigorous massage over an infected wound.\r\n* Modify or postpone treatment if the patient is systemically unwell.\r\n* Maintain mobility of unaffected areas.\r\n* Follow wound-care and surgical precautions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Atelectasis\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Atelectasis is partial or complete collapse of alveoli, resulting in reduced ventilation of the affected lung area.\r\n\r\nCauses\r\n\r\n* Shallow breathing due to pain.\r\n* Anesthesia.\r\n* Prolonged bed rest.\r\n* Retained secretions.\r\n* Abdominal or thoracic surgery.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Breathlessness.\r\n* Reduced oxygen saturation.\r\n* Reduced chest expansion.\r\n* Decreased breath sounds.\r\n* Tachypnea.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\n* Upright positioning.\r\n* Deep breathing/thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Airway-clearance techniques when clinically indicated.\r\n* Monitor SpO₂ and respiratory status.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Pneumonia\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Pneumonia is an infection causing inflammation of the lung parenchyma/alveoli, often with consolidation and impaired gas exchange.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Fever.\r\n* Cough.\r\n* Sputum production.\r\n* Breathlessness.\r\n* Chest discomfort.\r\n* Reduced oxygen saturation.\r\n* Fatigue.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Positioning to optimize ventilation.\r\n* Breathing exercises.\r\n* Airway-clearance techniques when indicated.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Progressive walking and exercise.\r\n* Monitor respiratory rate, HR and SpO₂.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Urinary Tract Infection (UTI)\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* UTI is an infection affecting any part of the urinary tract, including the bladder, urethra, ureters or kidneys.\r\n\r\nPostoperative risk factors\r\n\r\n* Urinary catheterization.\r\n* Prolonged catheter use.\r\n* Immobility.\r\n* Inadequate hydration.\r\n* Urinary retention.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Burning or pain during urination.\r\n* Increased urinary frequency.\r\n* Urgency.\r\n* Lower abdominal discomfort.\r\n* Fever in more significant infection.\r\n* Cloudy or foul-smelling urine.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy implications\r\n\r\n* Encourage early mobilization when appropriate.\r\n* Encourage adequate mobility and hydration according to medical advice.\r\n* Maintain catheter safety during transfers and walking.\r\n* Maintain infection-control measures.\r\n* Monitor for fever or systemic deterioration.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Pressure Sores / Pressure Injuries\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* A pressure injury is localized damage to the skin and/or underlying tissue, usually occurring over a bony prominence due to prolonged pressure or pressure combined with shear.\r\n\r\nRisk factors\r\n\r\n* Prolonged bed rest.\r\n* Reduced consciousness.\r\n* Poor nutrition.\r\n* Incontinence.\r\n* Reduced sensation.\r\n* Immobility.\r\n\r\nCommon sites\r\n\r\n* Sacrum.\r\n* Heels.\r\n* Greater trochanters.\r\n* Elbows.\r\n* Occiput.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\n* Regular repositioning.\r\n* Pressure-relieving positions and surfaces.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* ROM exercises.\r\n* Reduce prolonged pressure over bony prominences.\r\n* Strengthening and functional mobility.\r\n* Patient and caregiver education.\r\n* Coordinate with the wound-care team.///////////////////////Physiotherapy Management After Surgery—\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Physiotherapy management after surgery is the planned use of physiotherapy assessment, exercises, positioning, mobilization and education to prevent postoperative complications, restore physical function and promote safe recovery.\r\n\r\nMain Physiotherapy Management\r\n\r\n1. Assessment\r\n\r\n* Check vital signs: HR, BP, RR and SpO₂.\r\n* Assess pain.\r\n* Assess respiratory status.\r\n* Assess ROM and muscle strength.\r\n* Assess bed mobility, transfers and gait.\r\n* Check surgical wound and precautions.\r\n\r\n2. Respiratory Physiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Airway-clearance techniques when indicated.\r\n* Appropriate positioning.\r\n* Helps prevent atelectasis and pneumonia.\r\n\r\n3. Circulatory Exercises\r\n\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Active limb movements.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Helps reduce venous stasis and risk of DVT.\r\n\r\n4. Early Mobilization\r\n\r\n* Bed mobility.\r\n* Sitting at bedside.\r\n* Sit-to-stand.\r\n* Transfer training.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Prevents deconditioning, weakness and pressure injuries.\r\n\r\n5. ROM and Strengthening\r\n\r\n* Passive/active ROM as appropriate.\r\n* Isometric exercises when indicated.\r\n* Progressive strengthening.\r\n* Prevents stiffness, muscle weakness and contractures.\r\n\r\n6. Patient Education\r\n\r\n* Teach safe bed mobility and log rolling when appropriate.\r\n* Wound protection during coughing and movement.\r\n* Posture and body mechanics.\r\n* Activity progression and home exercises.\r\n* Educate about warning signs requiring medical review.\r\n\r\nGoals\r\n\r\n* Prevent postoperative complications.\r\n* Reduce pain and improve mobility.\r\n* Maintain muscle strength and ROM.\r\n* Improve respiratory and cardiovascular function.\r\n* Restore functional independence and ADLs.//////////////////Mastectomy, Complications and Physiotherapy Management—\r\n\r\n1. Definition of Mastectomy\r\n\r\n* Mastectomy is a surgical procedure in which part or all of the breast tissue is removed, most commonly for the treatment of breast cancer. \r\n* It may be performed on one breast (unilateral) or both breasts (bilateral).\r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* Simple/total mastectomy: removal of the whole breast.\r\n* Modified radical mastectomy: removal of the breast with axillary lymph-node dissection.\r\n* Skin-sparing mastectomy: breast tissue is removed while preserving most of the breast skin.\r\n* Nipple-sparing mastectomy: breast tissue is removed while preserving the nipple-areola complex.\r\n* Radical mastectomy: extensive removal of breast, chest-wall muscles and lymph nodes, now rarely performed.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Complications After Mastectomy\r\n\r\nEarly complications\r\n\r\n* Pain at the surgical site.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Bleeding or hematoma.\r\n* Seroma, which is accumulation of fluid around the surgical site. \r\n* Wound-healing problems.\r\n* Reduced shoulder movement.\r\n* Arm and shoulder stiffness.\r\n* Numbness or altered sensation around the chest or arm.\r\n* Fatigue.\r\n\r\nLate complications\r\n\r\n* Lymphedema of the arm, hand, breast or chest wall, particularly after lymph-node removal or radiotherapy. \r\n* Shoulder dysfunction or frozen shoulder.\r\n* Post-mastectomy pain syndrome.\r\n* Scar adhesion and tissue tightness.\r\n* Axillary web syndrome (cording).\r\n* Postural abnormalities, especially rounded shoulders.\r\n* Muscle weakness and reduced physical activity.\r\n* Reduced functional independence.\r\n* Psychological problems related to altered body image.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Physiotherapy Management After Mastectomy\r\n\r\nA. Physiotherapy Assessment\r\n\r\n* Assess pain and surgical wound.\r\n* Assess shoulder and upper-limb ROM.\r\n* Assess muscle strength.\r\n* Assess posture.\r\n* Assess sensation.\r\n* Assess upper-limb swelling and signs of lymphedema.\r\n* Assess respiratory status.\r\n* Assess functional activities and ADLs.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nB. Respiratory Physiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing when required.\r\n* Appropriate positioning.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Helps prevent respiratory complications after surgery.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nC. Shoulder and Upper-Limb Exercises\r\n\r\n* Begin gentle exercises according to surgical instructions.\r\n* Shoulder flexion exercises.\r\n* Shoulder abduction exercises.\r\n* Elbow flexion and extension.\r\n* Wrist and finger movements.\r\n* Scapular movements.\r\n* Gradually restore full shoulder ROM.\r\n\r\nBreast-surgery rehabilitation commonly includes postoperative shoulder exercises to maintain or restore arm mobility. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nD. Lymphedema Prevention and Management\r\n\r\n* Encourage regular, gradual arm and shoulder movement.\r\n* Avoid prolonged inactivity of the affected arm.\r\n* Maintain healthy body weight and regular physical activity.\r\n* Protect the skin from cuts, burns and infection.\r\n* Monitor for:\r\n    * Arm swelling.\r\n    * Heaviness.\r\n    * Tightness.\r\n    * Reduced ROM.\r\n* Refer to a specialist lymphedema service if lymphedema develops.\r\n\r\nFor established lymphedema, decongestive lymphatic therapy may include exercise, compression, skin care and specialist manual lymphatic drainage. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nE. Postural Correction\r\n\r\n* Encourage upright sitting and standing.\r\n* Avoid rounded shoulders.\r\n* Perform scapular retraction exercises.\r\n* Maintain normal thoracic mobility.\r\n* Correct protective postures caused by pain.\r\n\r\nGood posture helps maintain shoulder function and may reduce muscular tension. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nF. Scar Management\r\n\r\n* Once the wound is fully healed, scar mobility can be assessed.\r\n* Gentle scar mobilization may be used when appropriate.\r\n* Stretching exercises can help reduce tissue tightness.\r\n* Monitor for excessive scar formation or adhesions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nG. Functional Rehabilitation\r\n\r\n* Bed mobility training.\r\n* Transfer training.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Upper-limb functional activities.\r\n* Progressive strengthening.\r\n* Gradual return to ADLs.\r\n* Gradual return to work, exercise and recreational activities according to medical clearance.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nH. Patient Education\r\n\r\n* Follow wound-care instructions.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting and strenuous activity until medically cleared.\r\n* Perform prescribed exercises regularly.\r\n* Protect the affected arm from cuts, burns and infection.\r\n* Maintain good skin care.\r\n* Recognize early signs of lymphedema.\r\n* Maintain regular physical activity.\r\n* Seek medical advice for increasing swelling, redness, wound discharge or severe pain.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Physiotherapy Goals\r\n\r\n* Reduce pain and stiffness.\r\n* Restore shoulder and upper-limb ROM.\r\n* Maintain muscle strength.\r\n* Prevent or manage lymphedema.\r\n* Prevent postural abnormalities.\r\n* Improve functional independence.\r\n* Prevent complications of immobility.\r\n* Improve quality of life and return to normal activities.////////////////////Amputation\r\n\r\nDefinition, Indications, Levels, Complications, Stump Care, Phantom Limb, Prosthetic Rehabilitation and Cineplasty—\r\n\r\n1. Definition\r\n\r\n* Amputation is the surgical removal of part or all of a limb or extremity because of disease, trauma or other conditions in which the limb cannot be preserved.\r\n* The remaining portion of the limb is called the residual limb or stump. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Indications of Amputation\r\n\r\n* Severe trauma with an unsalvageable limb.\r\n* Severe peripheral vascular disease/ischemia.\r\n* Diabetic foot with irreversible tissue damage.\r\n* Gangrene.\r\n* Severe infection or uncontrolled osteomyelitis.\r\n* Malignant bone or soft-tissue tumour.\r\n* Congenital limb deformity in selected cases.\r\n* Severe burns or frostbite with non-viable tissue.\r\n* Failed previous reconstruction or repeated infection.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Levels of Amputation\r\n\r\nLower limb\r\n\r\n* Hemipelvectomy.\r\n* Hip disarticulation.\r\n* Transfemoral amputation - above knee.\r\n* Knee disarticulation.\r\n* Transtibial amputation - below knee.\r\n* Syme’s amputation.\r\n* Ankle/foot amputation.\r\n* Partial foot/toe amputation.\r\n\r\nUpper limb\r\n\r\n* Forequarter amputation.\r\n* Shoulder disarticulation.\r\n* Transhumeral amputation - above elbow.\r\n* Elbow disarticulation.\r\n* Transradial amputation - below elbow.\r\n* Wrist disarticulation.\r\n* Partial hand/finger amputation.\r\n\r\nPrinciple: Preserve the maximum useful limb length and, whenever possible, preserve important joints to optimize functional and prosthetic outcomes. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Complications of Amputation\r\n\r\nEarly complications\r\n\r\n* Hemorrhage.\r\n* Hematoma.\r\n* Infection.\r\n* Wound breakdown.\r\n* Edema.\r\n* Skin necrosis.\r\n* Pain.\r\n* Poor wound healing.\r\n\r\nLate complications\r\n\r\n* Joint contracture.\r\n* Muscle weakness.\r\n* Residual-limb pain.\r\n* Neuroma.\r\n* Phantom limb sensation.\r\n* Phantom limb pain.\r\n* Poor prosthetic fit.\r\n* Skin problems due to prosthetic pressure.\r\n* Reduced mobility and functional independence. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Stump / Residual-Limb Care\r\n\r\n* Inspect the stump daily for redness, swelling, pressure areas and skin breakdown.\r\n* Maintain good stump hygiene.\r\n* Keep the healed residual limb clean and dry.\r\n* Control edema using appropriate compression/shrinker techniques when prescribed.\r\n* Shape the stump appropriately for prosthetic fitting.\r\n* Use correct positioning to prevent contractures.\r\n* Perform regular ROM exercises.\r\n* Strengthen the residual limb and remaining muscles.\r\n* Perform desensitization when appropriate.\r\n* Care for the scar after adequate wound healing.\r\n* Avoid excessive pressure or friction.\r\n* Protect the opposite limb, particularly in patients with vascular disease or diabetes. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Phantom Limb\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Phantom limb sensation is the feeling that the amputated limb is still present.\r\n* Phantom limb pain (PLP) is painful sensation perceived in the missing limb.\r\n\r\nFeatures\r\n\r\n* Burning pain.\r\n* Shooting or electric-like pain.\r\n* Cramping.\r\n* Tingling.\r\n* Feeling of movement or position of the missing limb.\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Patient education and reassurance.\r\n* Desensitization techniques.\r\n* Gentle tapping and massage of the healed residual limb.\r\n* Graded motor imagery.\r\n* Mirror therapy may be used as part of rehabilitation.\r\n* Appropriate prosthetic use when suitable.\r\n* Psychological support when required.\r\n* Medical management of pain when necessary. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Prosthetic Rehabilitation\r\n\r\nA. Pre-prosthetic phase\r\n\r\n* Control edema.\r\n* Shape the residual limb.\r\n* Maintain ROM.\r\n* Prevent contractures.\r\n* Strengthen residual and intact limbs.\r\n* Improve balance.\r\n* Improve cardiovascular endurance.\r\n* Desensitize the residual limb.\r\n* Train transfers and mobility.\r\n* Teach ADLs.\r\n\r\nB. Prosthetic fitting\r\n\r\n* Assess residual-limb condition.\r\n* Select an appropriate prosthesis.\r\n* Ensure proper socket fit.\r\n* Teach donning and doffing.\r\n* Teach prosthetic hygiene and skin inspection.\r\n* Gradually increase wearing time. \r\n\r\nC. Prosthetic training\r\n\r\n* Weight shifting.\r\n* Standing balance.\r\n* Parallel-bar training.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Walking with assistive devices initially when required.\r\n* Stair training.\r\n* Uneven-surface training.\r\n* Functional and community mobility.\r\n\r\nD. Follow-up\r\n\r\n* Regular assessment of skin and residual limb.\r\n* Prosthetic adjustment as limb volume changes.\r\n* Gait assessment.\r\n* Maintenance of prosthetic components.\r\n* Long-term functional and psychological support. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Cineplasty\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Cineplasty is a surgical technique in which a muscle-tendon unit of the residual limb is brought through the stump and attached to a mechanical control system, allowing the patient’s muscle contraction to operate or control a prosthetic device.\r\n\r\nPrinciple\r\n\r\nMuscle contraction → movement of control mechanism → prosthetic movement\r\n\r\nAdvantages\r\n\r\n* Provides more direct control of the prosthesis.\r\n* Provides sensory feedback through muscle activity.\r\n* May improve functional control of some prosthetic devices.\r\n* Can be particularly useful in selected upper-limb amputees.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Physiotherapy Goals After Amputation\r\n\r\n* Reduce pain and edema.\r\n* Prevent contractures.\r\n* Maintain and improve muscle strength.\r\n* Improve balance and coordination.\r\n* Develop independence in transfers and ADLs.\r\n* Prepare the residual limb for prosthetic fitting.\r\n* Train safe prosthetic use.\r\n* Restore gait and functional mobility.\r\n* Promote psychological adjustment and community reintegration.///////////////////////Vascular Disorders—\r\n\r\nVaricose Veins, Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease, DVT and Surgical Management\r\n\r\n1. Varicose Veins\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Varicose veins are dilated, elongated and tortuous superficial veins, most commonly in the lower limbs, caused by failure of venous valves leading to venous reflux and pooling of blood. \r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Visible, twisted and enlarged veins.\r\n* Leg pain or aching.\r\n* Heaviness and fatigue of legs.\r\n* Ankle swelling.\r\n* Itching.\r\n* Skin pigmentation or eczema in chronic disease.\r\n* Venous ulceration in severe cases.\r\n* Superficial thrombophlebitis may occur.\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Regular exercise and weight management.\r\n* Leg elevation.\r\n* Compression therapy when appropriate.\r\n* Endothermal ablation.\r\n* Ultrasound-guided foam sclerotherapy.\r\n* Surgical ligation/stripping when other interventions are unsuitable. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease (PAOD)\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Peripheral arterial occlusive disease is a condition in which arterial blood flow to the limbs is reduced due to narrowing or blockage of peripheral arteries, most commonly because of atherosclerosis.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Intermittent claudication: pain in the limb during walking that improves with rest.\r\n* Cold extremity.\r\n* Pale or bluish skin.\r\n* Reduced or absent peripheral pulses.\r\n* Shiny, thin skin.\r\n* Loss of hair.\r\n* Delayed wound healing.\r\n* Ischemic ulcers.\r\n* Severe disease may cause rest pain and gangrene.\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Smoking cessation.\r\n* Regular/supervised exercise.\r\n* Control of diabetes, hypertension and cholesterol.\r\n* Antiplatelet and lipid-lowering medication as prescribed.\r\n* Angioplasty/stenting for selected lesions.\r\n* Arterial bypass surgery when indicated.\r\n* Amputation may be required for irreversible tissue loss/gangrene. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* DVT is the formation of a blood clot within a deep vein, usually in the lower limb, thigh or pelvis. \r\n\r\nRisk factors\r\n\r\n* Prolonged immobilization.\r\n* Major surgery.\r\n* Trauma.\r\n* Cancer.\r\n* Previous DVT.\r\n* Pregnancy.\r\n* Obesity.\r\n* Prolonged sitting or bed rest.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Unilateral leg swelling.\r\n* Calf or thigh pain/tenderness.\r\n* Warmth.\r\n* Redness or discoloration.\r\n* Increased limb circumference.\r\n* Some patients may have no symptoms. \r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Pulmonary embolism (PE).\r\n* Post-thrombotic syndrome.\r\n* Chronic swelling and pain.\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Anticoagulant therapy is the main treatment.\r\n* Compression may help selected patients with symptoms.\r\n* Leg elevation for swelling.\r\n* Early walking/mobilization when medically appropriate.\r\n* Selected extensive DVTs may require catheter-based clot removal. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precaution\r\n\r\n* Do not perform vigorous massage over a suspected DVT.\r\n* Suspected DVT requires prompt medical assessment.\r\n* Mobilization should follow the treating team’s instructions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Surgical Management of Vascular Disorders\r\n\r\nSurgical treatment depends on whether the disorder is venous or arterial.\r\n\r\nA. Varicose veins\r\n\r\n* Endovenous thermal ablation.\r\n* Ultrasound-guided foam sclerotherapy.\r\n* Surgical ligation and stripping when indicated.\r\n* Phlebectomy for selected tributaries. \r\n\r\nB. Peripheral arterial occlusive disease\r\n\r\n* Angioplasty:\r\n    * Balloon catheter is used to widen the narrowed artery.\r\n* Stent placement:\r\n    * A stent may be inserted to maintain arterial patency.\r\n* Arterial bypass grafting:\r\n    * A graft creates an alternative route around the blocked artery.\r\n* Thrombectomy/embolectomy may be performed for selected acute arterial occlusions.\r\n* Amputation may be required when severe ischemia has resulted in irreversible tissue loss. \r\n\r\nC. Aneurysms\r\n\r\n* Endovascular stent-graft repair.\r\n* Open surgical aneurysm repair.\r\n\r\nD. Severe DVT\r\n\r\n* Anticoagulation is generally first-line treatment.\r\n* Selected patients with extensive iliofemoral DVT may undergo catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Physiotherapy Implications\r\n\r\n* Assess peripheral pulses, skin condition, pain, swelling and functional mobility.\r\n* Encourage graded exercise in stable peripheral arterial disease.\r\n* Promote early mobilization after vascular surgery when medically appropriate.\r\n* Provide ankle and lower-limb exercises.\r\n* Monitor wound healing.\r\n* Educate regarding foot and skin care.\r\n* Avoid excessive pressure or trauma to ischemic tissues.\r\n* Avoid vigorous massage when DVT is suspected.\r\n* After amputation, provide stump care, strengthening, balance and prosthetic rehabilitation.////////////////Wound – Wound Healing, Factors, Types, Non-Healing Wounds & Surgical Wound Management—-\r\n\r\n1. Definition of Wound\r\n\r\n* A wound is a disruption or break in the normal continuity and integrity of the skin, mucous membrane or underlying tissues caused by trauma, surgery, disease or other factors.\r\n* A wound may be open or closed, acute or chronic.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Wound Healing\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Wound healing is the natural biological process by which the body repairs damaged tissue and restores tissue continuity and function. \r\n\r\nPhases of Wound Healing\r\n\r\n1. Hemostasis\r\n    * Occurs immediately after injury.\r\n    * Vasoconstriction occurs.\r\n    * Platelets aggregate.\r\n    * Fibrin clot is formed.\r\n    * Stops bleeding.\r\n2. Inflammation\r\n    * Neutrophils and macrophages enter the wound.\r\n    * Removes bacteria, dead tissue and debris.\r\n    * Produces inflammatory mediators.\r\n3. Proliferation\r\n    * Fibroblasts produce collagen.\r\n    * Granulation tissue develops.\r\n    * New blood vessels form by angiogenesis.\r\n    * Re-epithelialization occurs.\r\n    * Wound contraction occurs.\r\n4. Remodeling/Maturation\r\n    * Collagen is reorganized.\r\n    * Scar tissue matures.\r\n    * Tensile strength gradually increases. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Factors Affecting Wound Healing\r\n\r\nA. Local Factors\r\n\r\n* Infection.\r\n* Poor blood supply/ischemia.\r\n* Hypoxia.\r\n* Excessive edema.\r\n* Wound size and depth.\r\n* Foreign bodies.\r\n* Repeated trauma or pressure.\r\n* Excessive wound tension.\r\n* Poor wound hygiene.\r\n* Tissue necrosis.\r\n\r\nB. Systemic Factors\r\n\r\n* Age.\r\n* Malnutrition and protein deficiency.\r\n* Vitamin and mineral deficiencies.\r\n* Diabetes mellitus.\r\n* Anemia.\r\n* Smoking.\r\n* Obesity.\r\n* Immunosuppression.\r\n* Poor circulation.\r\n* Systemic infection.\r\n* Corticosteroid therapy.\r\n* Chemotherapy and radiotherapy. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Types of Wounds\r\n\r\nA. According to duration\r\n\r\n* Acute wound\r\n    * Develops suddenly.\r\n    * Usually progresses through normal healing.\r\n    * Example: surgical incision, laceration.\r\n* Chronic/non-healing wound\r\n    * Fails to progress normally through healing.\r\n    * Example: diabetic ulcer, venous ulcer, pressure ulcer.\r\n\r\nB. According to cause\r\n\r\n* Surgical wound.\r\n* Traumatic wound.\r\n* Burn wound.\r\n* Pressure wound.\r\n* Diabetic wound.\r\n* Vascular ulcer.\r\n\r\nC. According to skin integrity\r\n\r\n* Open wound\r\n    * Skin is broken.\r\n    * Example: laceration, abrasion.\r\n* Closed wound\r\n    * Skin remains intact but underlying tissues are damaged.\r\n    * Example: contusion or hematoma.\r\n\r\nD. According to contamination\r\n\r\n* Clean wound.\r\n* Clean-contaminated wound.\r\n* Contaminated wound.\r\n* Dirty/infected wound. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Non-Healing Wounds\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* A non-healing or chronic wound is a wound that fails to progress through the normal healing process and remains open or recurrent.\r\n* Chronic wounds commonly include diabetic ulcers, venous ulcers, arterial ulcers and pressure ulcers. \r\n\r\nCauses\r\n\r\n* Diabetes mellitus.\r\n* Peripheral arterial disease.\r\n* Venous insufficiency.\r\n* Pressure.\r\n* Infection.\r\n* Poor nutrition.\r\n* Smoking.\r\n* Poor oxygenation.\r\n* Edema.\r\n* Neuropathy.\r\n* Immunosuppression.\r\n* Repeated trauma.\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Persistent wound.\r\n* Delayed or absent epithelialization.\r\n* Excessive or abnormal discharge.\r\n* Slough or necrotic tissue.\r\n* Poor granulation tissue.\r\n* Recurrent infection.\r\n* Wound enlargement.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Surgical Wound Management\r\n\r\nA. Assessment\r\n\r\n* Assess wound location and size.\r\n* Observe wound edges.\r\n* Check colour and condition of wound bed.\r\n* Assess exudate/discharge.\r\n* Check for redness, swelling, warmth and pain.\r\n* Assess for wound dehiscence.\r\n* Monitor temperature and systemic signs of infection.\r\n\r\nB. Wound Care\r\n\r\n* Maintain aseptic technique during dressing changes.\r\n* Keep the wound appropriately protected.\r\n* Use appropriate dressings according to wound type.\r\n* Clean the wound according to clinical protocol.\r\n* Sterile saline is recommended for cleansing surgical wounds in the early postoperative period according to NICE guidance. \r\n\r\nC. Infection Prevention\r\n\r\n* Proper hand hygiene.\r\n* Aseptic non-touch technique.\r\n* Appropriate wound dressing.\r\n* Early recognition of infection.\r\n* Antibiotics when clinically indicated for surgical-site infection.\r\n* Report increasing redness, pain, swelling, purulent discharge or fever. \r\n\r\nD. Promote Healing\r\n\r\n* Maintain adequate nutrition and hydration.\r\n* Maintain adequate oxygenation and circulation.\r\n* Control blood glucose in diabetic patients.\r\n* Reduce pressure on the wound.\r\n* Control edema.\r\n* Encourage appropriate physical activity and mobility.\r\n\r\nE. Physiotherapy Management\r\n\r\n* Appropriate positioning.\r\n* Pressure-relieving techniques.\r\n* Early mobilization when medically safe.\r\n* ROM exercises.\r\n* Muscle strengthening.\r\n* Breathing exercises after major surgery.\r\n* Circulatory exercises.\r\n* Functional mobility and gait training.\r\n* Patient education regarding wound protection.\r\n* Scar management only after adequate wound closure/healing.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Complications of Wounds\r\n\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Hematoma.\r\n* Seroma.\r\n* Wound dehiscence.\r\n* Necrosis.\r\n* Delayed healing.\r\n* Chronic ulceration.\r\n* Excessive scar formation.\r\n* Contracture.\r\n* Sepsis in severe infection.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nFlowchart of Wound Healing\r\n\r\nWound/Injury\r\n↓\r\nHemostasis\r\n↓\r\nInflammation\r\n↓\r\nProliferation\r\n↓\r\nRemodeling/Maturation\r\n↓\r\nScar formation and tissue strengthening//////////////////////Fluid & Electrolytes\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Definition of Fluid and Electrolytes\r\n\r\n* Body fluid is the water present in the body that forms the major component of blood, cells and extracellular spaces.\r\n* Electrolytes are minerals that carry an electrical charge when dissolved in body fluids.\r\n* Important electrolytes include:\r\n    * Sodium (Na⁺)\r\n    * Potassium (K⁺)\r\n    * Calcium (Ca²⁺)\r\n    * Chloride (Cl⁻)\r\n    * Magnesium (Mg²⁺)\r\n    * Phosphate (PO₄³⁻)\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Fluid Balance\r\n\r\n* Fluid balance is the maintenance of a relatively stable relationship between fluid intake and fluid output.\r\n* Fluid intake: drinking, food, enteral feeding and IV fluids.\r\n* Fluid output: urine, sweat, stool, respiration and other losses.\r\n* Normal fluid balance is important for:\r\n    * Blood circulation.\r\n    * Blood pressure.\r\n    * Cellular function.\r\n    * Temperature regulation.\r\n    * Electrolyte balance.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Dehydration\r\n\r\n* Dehydration is a condition in which the body loses more water than it takes in, resulting in insufficient body water.\r\n* Causes:\r\n    * Vomiting.\r\n    * Diarrhea.\r\n    * Excessive sweating.\r\n    * Fever.\r\n    * Inadequate fluid intake.\r\n    * Blood/fluid loss.\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Thirst.\r\n    * Dry mouth.\r\n    * Reduced urine output.\r\n    * Dark urine.\r\n    * Dizziness.\r\n    * Weakness.\r\n    * Tachycardia.\r\n    * Hypotension in severe cases.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Electrolyte Imbalance\r\n\r\n* Electrolyte imbalance is an abnormal increase or decrease in the concentration of one or more electrolytes in body fluids.\r\n* Common abnormalities:\r\n    * Hyponatremia / hypernatremia\r\n    * Hypokalemia / hyperkalemia\r\n    * Hypocalcemia / hypercalcemia\r\n    * Hypomagnesemia / hypermagnesemia\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Muscle weakness.\r\n    * Cramps.\r\n    * Fatigue.\r\n    * Confusion.\r\n    * Nausea/vomiting.\r\n    * Abnormal heart rhythm, particularly with significant potassium abnormalities.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Management\r\n\r\n* Identify and treat the underlying cause.\r\n* Monitor:\r\n    * Fluid intake/output.\r\n    * Blood pressure.\r\n    * Heart rate.\r\n    * Urine output.\r\n    * Serum electrolytes.\r\n* Mild dehydration: oral fluids or oral rehydration solution when appropriate.\r\n* Severe dehydration: IV fluid replacement, usually with appropriate isotonic crystalloid depending on the clinical situation.\r\n* Replace deficient electrolytes appropriately.\r\n* Restrict fluids when clinically indicated for specific fluid-overload conditions.\r\n* Avoid rapid or excessive correction of electrolyte abnormalities.\r\n* Monitor the patient’s clinical response.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy Precaution\r\n\r\n* Check the patient’s vital signs and medical stability before treatment.\r\n* Modify or postpone intensive physiotherapy in significant dehydration or electrolyte disturbance.\r\n* Encourage appropriate mobility and hydration according to the medical plan./////////////////////////////Drains and Tubes: ICD, Foley Catheter, Hemovac, CVC & PICC\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Drains – Definition\r\n\r\n* A surgical drain is a tube or device inserted into a body cavity, wound or surgical site to remove blood, pus, serous fluid, bile or other unwanted collections.\r\n* Drains help prevent fluid accumulation and may reduce complications such as infection or hematoma.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. ICD – Intercostal Drain\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* An intercostal drain (ICD) is a tube inserted through the chest wall into the pleural cavity to remove air, blood or fluid and allow the lung to re-expand.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Pneumothorax.\r\n* Hemothorax.\r\n* Pleural effusion.\r\n* Empyema.\r\n* Post-thoracic surgery.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precautions\r\n\r\n* Check the drain system before treatment.\r\n* Keep the drainage bottle/system upright and below chest level.\r\n* Avoid pulling, kinking or compressing the tube.\r\n* Encourage breathing exercises and early mobilization when medically stable.\r\n* Monitor SpO₂, respiratory rate and symptoms.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Foley Catheter\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* A Foley catheter is an indwelling urinary catheter inserted through the urethra into the urinary bladder to continuously drain urine.\r\n* A balloon near the tip keeps the catheter in place.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Urinary retention.\r\n* Accurate urine-output monitoring in selected critically ill patients.\r\n* Perioperative bladder drainage.\r\n* Certain urological conditions.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precautions\r\n\r\n* Keep the drainage bag below bladder level.\r\n* Avoid kinking or pulling the catheter.\r\n* Secure the catheter during mobilization.\r\n* Encourage early mobility when appropriate.\r\n* Maintain infection-control precautions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Hemovac\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Hemovac is a closed-suction drainage system used to remove blood and other fluid from a surgical wound.\r\n\r\nUses\r\n\r\n* Orthopaedic surgery.\r\n* Joint replacement.\r\n* Spinal surgery.\r\n* Other operations where postoperative fluid collection is expected.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precautions\r\n\r\n* Check the drain and suction reservoir before treatment.\r\n* Keep the reservoir secured.\r\n* Avoid pulling or compressing the tubing.\r\n* Monitor the amount and appearance of drainage.\r\n* Mobilize carefully without tension on the drain.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. CVC – Central Venous Catheter\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* A central venous catheter (CVC) is a catheter whose tip lies in a large central vein, commonly the superior vena cava, for administration of medications, fluids or other therapies.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Administration of irritating/vesicant medications.\r\n* Long-term IV therapy.\r\n* Administration of parenteral nutrition.\r\n* Hemodynamic monitoring in selected patients.\r\n* Poor peripheral venous access.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precautions\r\n\r\n* Ensure the catheter is secure before movement.\r\n* Avoid excessive traction on the catheter.\r\n* Monitor the insertion site for redness, swelling or bleeding.\r\n* Observe for signs of infection or catheter complications.\r\n* Coordinate with nursing/medical staff during mobilization.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. PICC – Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* A PICC is a long, flexible catheter inserted through a peripheral vein, usually in the upper arm, with its tip advanced into a central vein.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Long-term IV antibiotics.\r\n* Long-term medication administration.\r\n* Chemotherapy.\r\n* Parenteral nutrition.\r\n* Repeated blood sampling in selected patients.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy precautions\r\n\r\n* Keep the PICC secure.\r\n* Avoid pulling or excessive pressure on the catheter.\r\n* Follow activity restrictions provided by the medical team.\r\n* Monitor the arm for:\r\n    * Swelling.\r\n    * Pain.\r\n    * Redness.\r\n    * Bleeding.\r\n* Stop treatment and report suspected catheter complications.////////////////////Abdominal & Pelvic Surgeries\r\n\r\nAppendectomy, Herniorrhaphy, Cholecystectomy, Hysterectomy, Colostomy & Ileostomy\r\n\r\nPoint-wise format for Physiotherapy Surgery Exam\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. APPENDECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Appendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix.\r\n* It is most commonly performed for acute appendicitis. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Acute uncomplicated appendicitis.\r\n* Complicated appendicitis.\r\n* Gangrenous appendix.\r\n* Perforated appendix.\r\n* Appendicular abscess in selected cases.\r\n* Generalized peritonitis due to perforation.\r\n* Appendiceal neoplasm in selected cases. \r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\nLaparoscopic appendectomy\r\n\r\n1. General anesthesia is administered.\r\n2. Patient is positioned supine.\r\n3. Abdomen is prepared and draped.\r\n4. Pneumoperitoneum is created using CO₂.\r\n5. Laparoscopic ports are inserted.\r\n6. Cecum and appendix are identified.\r\n7. Appendix is mobilized.\r\n8. Mesoappendix and appendicular vessels are divided.\r\n9. Base of appendix is secured and divided.\r\n10. Appendix is removed, usually using a retrieval bag.\r\n11. Operative site is inspected for bleeding or contamination.\r\n12. CO₂ is released.\r\n13. Ports are removed and wounds are closed. \r\n\r\nFlow\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Pneumoperitoneum → Identify appendix → Divide mesoappendix → Divide appendix → Remove appendix → Check → Close\r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Intra-abdominal abscess.\r\n* Bleeding/hematoma.\r\n* Postoperative pain.\r\n* Ileus.\r\n* Adhesive intestinal obstruction.\r\n* Peritonitis/sepsis in complicated cases.\r\n* Stump appendicitis. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Assess vital signs, pain and respiratory status.\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Teach log rolling for bed mobility when appropriate.\r\n* Protect the abdominal wound.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting/strenuous activity until medically cleared.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. HERNIORRHAPHY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Herniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by reducing the herniated contents and repairing the abdominal-wall defect.\r\n* Modern repairs frequently use mesh reinforcement when appropriate. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Symptomatic hernia.\r\n* Painful hernia.\r\n* Enlarging hernia.\r\n* Irreducible/incarcerated hernia.\r\n* Strangulated hernia.\r\n* Intestinal obstruction caused by hernia.\r\n* Recurrent hernia.\r\n* Risk of future complications in selected patients. \r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. Patient is assessed and prepared.\r\n2. Appropriate anesthesia is administered.\r\n3. Incision is made over the hernia.\r\n4. Hernia sac is identified.\r\n5. Sac is dissected from surrounding structures.\r\n6. Herniated bowel/omentum is inspected.\r\n7. Viable contents are reduced into the abdominal cavity.\r\n8. Non-viable bowel may require resection.\r\n9. Hernia sac is reduced or excised.\r\n10. Fascial defect is repaired.\r\n11. Mesh may be placed for reinforcement.\r\n12. Hemostasis is achieved.\r\n13. Wound is closed in layers. \r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Hematoma.\r\n* Seroma.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Chronic postoperative pain.\r\n* Urinary retention.\r\n* Nerve injury.\r\n* Injury to bowel, bladder or blood vessels.\r\n* Mesh infection.\r\n* Recurrence of hernia. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Teach safe bed mobility.\r\n* Gradual abdominal strengthening after adequate healing and medical clearance.\r\n* Avoid excessive straining and heavy lifting during the healing period.\r\n* Educate regarding posture and body mechanics.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. CHOLECYSTECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Cholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder.\r\n* It is commonly performed laparoscopically.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Symptomatic gallstones.\r\n* Acute cholecystitis.\r\n* Chronic cholecystitis.\r\n* Gallstone-related complications.\r\n* Gallstone pancreatitis in appropriate cases.\r\n* Selected gallbladder polyps or tumours.\r\n* Gallbladder trauma in selected cases. \r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\nLaparoscopic cholecystectomy\r\n\r\n1. General anesthesia is administered.\r\n2. Patient is positioned appropriately.\r\n3. Abdomen is prepared and draped.\r\n4. Pneumoperitoneum is created with CO₂.\r\n5. Laparoscopic ports are inserted.\r\n6. Gallbladder is exposed and retracted.\r\n7. Hepatocystic/Calot’s triangle is exposed.\r\n8. Cystic duct and cystic artery are identified.\r\n9. Critical view of safety is obtained.\r\n10. Cystic duct and artery are clipped/ligated and divided.\r\n11. Gallbladder is dissected from the liver bed.\r\n12. Gallbladder is removed through a port.\r\n13. Hemostasis and bile leakage are checked.\r\n14. Pneumoperitoneum is released.\r\n15. Ports are removed and wounds are closed. \r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Bleeding.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Bile leak.\r\n* Bile duct injury.\r\n* Injury to surrounding organs.\r\n* Retained common bile duct stone.\r\n* Postoperative nausea/vomiting.\r\n* Conversion to open surgery when required. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Assess respiratory status.\r\n* Deep breathing and thoracic expansion exercises.\r\n* Supported coughing.\r\n* Appropriate positioning.\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Gentle ROM.\r\n* Wound protection.\r\n* Gradual return to functional activities.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. HYSTERECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Hysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus.\r\n* Depending on the procedure, the cervix, fallopian tubes and ovaries may also be removed.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Abnormal uterine bleeding.\r\n* Uterine fibroids/leiomyoma.\r\n* Gynecological malignancy.\r\n* Premalignant disease.\r\n* Endometriosis.\r\n* Pelvic organ prolapse.\r\n* Chronic pelvic pain in selected cases.\r\n* Pelvic infection in selected cases. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* Total hysterectomy.\r\n* Subtotal/supracervical hysterectomy.\r\n* Radical hysterectomy.\r\n* Hysterectomy with removal of tubes and ovaries when indicated.\r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps — Abdominal Hysterectomy\r\n\r\n1. Patient is assessed and prepared.\r\n2. General anesthesia is administered.\r\n3. Patient is positioned appropriately.\r\n4. Abdomen is prepared and draped.\r\n5. Abdominal incision is made.\r\n6. Pelvic organs are inspected.\r\n7. Uterus is mobilized.\r\n8. Supporting ligaments are identified and divided.\r\n9. Uterine vessels are ligated and divided.\r\n10. Bladder is separated from the lower uterus/cervix.\r\n11. Uterus and cervix are removed.\r\n12. Vaginal cuff is closed.\r\n13. Hemostasis is checked.\r\n14. Abdominal wound is closed in layers.\r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Hemorrhage.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* UTI.\r\n* Pelvic infection/abscess.\r\n* DVT/VTE.\r\n* Bladder injury.\r\n* Ureteric injury.\r\n* Bowel injury.\r\n* Ileus.\r\n* Nerve injury.\r\n* Vaginal cuff dehiscence.\r\n* Urinary dysfunction. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Assess pain, respiratory status and mobility.\r\n* Deep breathing exercises.\r\n* Circulatory exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Postural education.\r\n* Pelvic-floor muscle exercises when appropriate.\r\n* Gradual abdominal strengthening.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting until medically cleared.\r\n* Educate regarding safe return to ADLs.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. COLOSTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma, allowing feces to leave the body through the stoma. \r\n* It may be temporary or permanent.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Colorectal cancer.\r\n* Bowel obstruction.\r\n* Bowel perforation.\r\n* Diverticular disease with obstruction.\r\n* Inflammatory bowel disease.\r\n* Traumatic bowel injury.\r\n* Protection of a distal bowel anastomosis.\r\n* Gangrenous sigmoid volvulus.\r\n* Severe perineal/anorectal disease.\r\n* Anastomotic leak.\r\n* Fecal diversion. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* End colostomy.\r\n* Loop colostomy.\r\n* Double-barrel colostomy.\r\n* Temporary or permanent colostomy.\r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. Patient is assessed and prepared.\r\n2. Stoma site is selected and marked.\r\n3. General anesthesia is administered.\r\n4. Abdomen is accessed by open or laparoscopic technique.\r\n5. Diseased bowel is identified.\r\n6. Bowel resection is performed if required.\r\n7. Appropriate colon segment is selected.\r\n8. Abdominal wall opening is created.\r\n9. Colon is brought through the opening without excessive tension.\r\n10. Colon is opened.\r\n11. Bowel edge is sutured to the skin.\r\n12. Stoma is matured.\r\n13. Abdominal wound is closed.\r\n14. Stoma appliance is applied. \r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Stoma ischemia/necrosis.\r\n* Stoma retraction.\r\n* Stoma prolapse.\r\n* Stenosis.\r\n* Peristomal skin irritation.\r\n* Parastomal hernia.\r\n* Bleeding.\r\n* Hematoma.\r\n* Bowel obstruction/ileus.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* DVT.\r\n* Atelectasis/pneumonia. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Respiratory exercises.\r\n* Deep breathing and supported coughing.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Ankle-pump exercises.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Avoid direct pressure over the stoma.\r\n* Protect the stoma appliance during movement.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Gradual strengthening.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal pressure in the early healing period.\r\n* Teach safe posture and body mechanics.\r\n* Coordinate with the stoma-care team.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. ILEOSTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Ileostomy is a procedure in which the ileum is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma, allowing intestinal contents to leave the body through the stoma. \r\n* It can be temporary or permanent and may be an end or loop ileostomy.\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Ulcerative colitis.\r\n* Crohn’s disease.\r\n* Colorectal cancer.\r\n* Total colectomy/proctocolectomy.\r\n* Severe bowel inflammation.\r\n* Bowel perforation.\r\n* Bowel ischemia.\r\n* Protection of a distal intestinal/colorectal anastomosis.\r\n* Anastomotic leak.\r\n* Severe perineal disease. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* End ileostomy.\r\n* Loop ileostomy.\r\n* Temporary ileostomy.\r\n* Permanent ileostomy.\r\n\r\nProcedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. Patient is assessed and prepared.\r\n2. Appropriate stoma site is selected and marked.\r\n3. General anesthesia is administered.\r\n4. Abdomen is accessed.\r\n5. Diseased bowel is identified and resected when required.\r\n6. Suitable ileal segment is selected.\r\n7. Opening is created in the abdominal wall.\r\n8. Ileum is brought through the opening.\r\n9. Ileum is checked for adequate blood supply and absence of tension.\r\n10. Ileum is opened.\r\n11. Stoma is everted/spouted to direct intestinal contents away from the skin.\r\n12. Ileum is sutured to the skin.\r\n13. Abdominal wound is closed.\r\n14. Stoma appliance is applied. \r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Dehydration.\r\n* Fluid and electrolyte imbalance.\r\n* High-output ileostomy.\r\n* Stoma ischemia/necrosis.\r\n* Stoma retraction.\r\n* Stoma prolapse.\r\n* Stenosis.\r\n* Peristomal skin irritation.\r\n* Parastomal hernia.\r\n* Bleeding.\r\n* Obstruction.\r\n* Infection/abscess.\r\n* Renal impairment secondary to significant fluid loss. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\r\n\r\n* Assess general condition and hydration status.\r\n* Respiratory exercises.\r\n* Circulatory exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Progressive walking.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Protect the stoma during transfers and exercise.\r\n* Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during early healing.\r\n* Progressive strengthening.\r\n* Monitor fatigue and exercise tolerance.\r\n* Educate regarding safe activity and stoma protection.\r\n* Coordinate with the stoma-care team.///// /////////////////////1. CT vs MRI\r\n\r\nCT – Computed Tomography\r\n\r\n* Uses X-rays and computer processing to produce cross-sectional images.\r\n* Fast and widely available.\r\n* Excellent for:\r\n    * Acute intracranial hemorrhage.\r\n    * Head trauma.\r\n    * Skull fractures.\r\n    * Bone abnormalities.\r\n* Uses ionizing radiation.\r\n* Provides less soft-tissue contrast than MRI.\r\n\r\nMRI – Magnetic Resonance Imaging\r\n\r\n* Uses a strong magnetic field and radiofrequency waves.\r\n* Does not use ionizing radiation.\r\n* Provides excellent soft-tissue contrast.\r\n* Better for:\r\n    * Brain and spinal cord lesions.\r\n    * Tumours.\r\n    * Demyelinating disease.\r\n    * Early ischemic stroke.\r\n    * Ligament, muscle and joint pathology.\r\n* More time-consuming and may be contraindicated or require special precautions with certain implants. 2. Indications of CT and MRI\r\n\r\nIndications of CT\r\n\r\n* Head injury and skull fracture.\r\n* Suspected intracranial hemorrhage.\r\n* Acute neurological emergencies.\r\n* Stroke assessment.\r\n* Bone pathology.\r\n* Chest and abdominal emergencies.\r\n* Detection of masses and structural abnormalities.\r\n\r\nIndications of MRI\r\n\r\n* Brain tumours.\r\n* Spinal cord and nerve lesions.\r\n* Multiple sclerosis/demyelination.\r\n* Early ischemic stroke.\r\n* Joint and soft-tissue injuries.\r\n* Ligament and tendon pathology.\r\n* Detailed assessment of brain and spinal lesions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. X-Ray Interpretation Basics\r\n\r\nA. Check the patient and film\r\n\r\n* Confirm patient identity.\r\n* Check the correct body part and side.\r\n* Identify the view/projection: AP, PA, lateral, etc.\r\n* Check image quality and positioning.\r\n\r\nB. Use a systematic approach\r\n\r\nFor a chest X-ray, a common approach is ABCDE:\r\n\r\n* A – Airway\r\n    * Tracheal position.\r\n    * Airway patency.\r\n* B – Breathing\r\n    * Lung fields.\r\n    * Pneumothorax.\r\n    * Consolidation.\r\n    * Pleural effusion.\r\n* C – Circulation\r\n    * Heart size and cardiac silhouette.\r\n    * Mediastinum.\r\n* D – Disability/diaphragm\r\n    * Diaphragms.\r\n    * Costophrenic angles.\r\n    * Check for free air where relevant.\r\n* E – Everything else\r\n    * Bones.\r\n    * Soft tissues.\r\n    * Lines, tubes and medical devices.\r\n\r\nImportant X-ray findings\r\n\r\n* Fracture: break/discontinuity in bone cortex.\r\n* Dislocation: abnormal relationship of joint surfaces.\r\n* Consolidation: increased lung opacity, often associated with infection.\r\n* Pneumothorax: pleural air with absent peripheral lung markings.\r\n* Pleural effusion: fluid causing blunting of the costophrenic angle.///////////////////////PAOD – Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Definition\r\n\r\n* Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease (PAOD) is a condition in which arterial blood flow to the peripheral limbs is reduced due to narrowing or blockage of arteries, most commonly due to atherosclerosis. (nhs.uk￼)\r\n\r\n2. Causes / Risk Factors\r\n\r\n* Atherosclerosis.\r\n* Smoking.\r\n* Diabetes mellitus.\r\n* Hypertension.\r\n* Hyperlipidemia.\r\n* Increasing age.\r\n* Obesity.\r\n* Family history of cardiovascular disease.\r\n\r\n3. Clinical Features\r\n\r\n* Intermittent claudication – pain/cramping in the limb during walking that improves with rest.\r\n* Cold extremity.\r\n* Pale or bluish skin.\r\n* Reduced/absent peripheral pulses.\r\n* Shiny, thin skin.\r\n* Loss of limb hair.\r\n* Delayed wound healing.\r\n* Ischemic ulcers.\r\n* Severe disease → rest pain and gangrene.\r\n\r\n4. Complications\r\n\r\n* Chronic ischemia.\r\n* Non-healing ulcers.\r\n* Tissue necrosis.\r\n* Gangrene.\r\n* Limb loss/amputation.\r\n* Increased cardiovascular risk, including heart attack and stroke. (nhs.uk￼)\r\n\r\n5. Management\r\n\r\n* Smoking cessation.\r\n* Supervised/structured exercise therapy.\r\n* Control of diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol.\r\n* Antiplatelet and lipid-lowering medication as prescribed.\r\n* Angioplasty/stenting for selected patients.\r\n* Arterial bypass surgery for selected severe disease.\r\n* Amputation may be required for irreversible tissue loss.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy Role\r\n\r\n* Graded walking/exercise programme as appropriate.\r\n* Lower-limb strengthening.\r\n* Functional mobility training.\r\n* Foot and skin-care education.\r\n* Monitor pain, skin condition and peripheral circulation.///////////////////Surgical Infections – Classification\r\n-\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Surgical infection is an infection involving a wound, tissue or organ that occurs before, during or after a surgical procedure and may interfere with normal healing.\r\n\r\nClassification of Surgical Infections\r\n\r\n1. According to Clinical Course\r\n\r\n* Acute surgical infections\r\n    * Develop rapidly.\r\n    * Usually associated with acute inflammation.\r\n    * Examples:\r\n        * Cellulitis\r\n        * Abscess\r\n        * Acute wound infection\r\n        * Necrotizing soft-tissue infection\r\n* Chronic surgical infections\r\n    * Persist for a prolonged period.\r\n    * May cause fibrosis, sinus formation or tissue destruction.\r\n    * Examples:\r\n        * Tuberculosis\r\n        * Chronic osteomyelitis\r\n        * Chronic wound infection\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. According to Site\r\n\r\n* Superficial infections\r\n    * Involve skin and subcutaneous tissue.\r\n    * Examples: cellulitis, superficial wound infection.\r\n* Deep infections\r\n    * Involve deep fascia, muscle or deeper tissues.\r\n    * Examples: deep incisional surgical-site infection, deep abscess.\r\n* Organ/space infections\r\n    * Involve an organ or body space related to surgery.\r\n    * Examples:\r\n        * Intra-abdominal abscess\r\n        * Pelvic abscess\r\n        * Postoperative pneumonia.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. According to Source\r\n\r\n* Exogenous infection\r\n    * Organisms enter from outside the patient’s body.\r\n    * Example: contamination from instruments, environment or healthcare personnel.\r\n* Endogenous infection\r\n    * Caused by microorganisms already present in the patient’s own body.\r\n    * Example: infection caused by intestinal flora following bowel surgery.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Surgical Site Infection (SSI) Classification\r\n\r\n* Superficial incisional SSI\r\n    * Involves skin and subcutaneous tissue.\r\n* Deep incisional SSI\r\n    * Involves deep soft tissues such as fascia and muscle.\r\n* Organ/space SSI\r\n    * Involves any part of the body deeper than the fascia/muscle that was opened or manipulated during surgery. (cdc.gov￼)\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. According to Presence of Pus\r\n\r\n* Non-suppurative infection\r\n    * Does not primarily produce pus.\r\n    * Example: cellulitis.\r\n* Suppurative infection\r\n    * Characterized by pus formation.\r\n    * Examples:\r\n        * Abscess\r\n        * Empyema\r\n        * Infected wound.////////////////////1. DVT – Deep Vein Thrombosis\r\n\r\n* Definition: Formation of a blood clot (thrombus) in a deep vein, usually of the lower limb.\r\n* Signs: Unilateral swelling, calf pain/tenderness, warmth and redness.\r\n* Complication: Pulmonary embolism.\r\n* Prevention: Early mobilization, ankle-pump exercises, compression devices and anticoagulants when prescribed.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Scar + Types\r\n\r\n* Definition: A scar is fibrous connective tissue formed during healing after tissue injury.\r\n* Types:\r\n    * Normal scar: Flat, soft and within wound margins.\r\n    * Hypertrophic scar: Raised but remains within wound margins.\r\n    * Keloid: Raised scar extending beyond original wound margins.\r\n    * Atrophic scar: Thin, depressed scar.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Types of Antibiotics\r\n\r\n* Cell-wall inhibitors: Penicillins, cephalosporins.\r\n* Protein-synthesis inhibitors: Aminoglycosides, macrolides, tetracyclines.\r\n* Nucleic-acid inhibitors: Fluoroquinolones, rifampicin.\r\n* Antimetabolites: Sulfonamides, trimethoprim.\r\n* Cell-membrane disruptors: Polymyxins.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Dehydration + Manifestations\r\n\r\n* Definition: Dehydration is a condition in which body water loss exceeds water intake.\r\n* Manifestations:\r\n    * Thirst.\r\n    * Dry mouth and mucous membranes.\r\n    * Reduced/dark urine.\r\n    * Weakness and dizziness.\r\n    * Tachycardia.\r\n    * Hypotension in severe cases.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Surgical Drains\r\n\r\n* Definition: Surgical drains are tubes/devices used to remove blood, pus, serous fluid or other unwanted collections from a wound or body cavity.\r\n* Examples:\r\n    * Intercostal drain (ICD).\r\n    * Hemovac.\r\n    * Jackson-Pratt drain.\r\n    * T-tube.\r\n* Purpose: Prevent fluid accumulation and facilitate healing.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Types of Anaesthesia\r\n\r\n* Local anaesthesia: Loss of sensation in a limited area without loss of consciousness.\r\n* Regional anaesthesia: Blocks sensation in a larger region.\r\n    * Example: spinal, epidural.\r\n* General anaesthesia: Drug-induced reversible unconsciousness with loss of protective reflexes and sensation.\r\n* Sedation: Reduced anxiety/awareness with varying levels of consciousness.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Types of Wounds\r\n\r\n* Open wound: Break in skin continuity.\r\n    * Example: laceration, abrasion.\r\n* Closed wound: Skin remains intact but underlying tissue is damaged.\r\n    * Example: contusion.\r\n* Acute wound: Usually heals in an expected time.\r\n    * Example: surgical incision.\r\n* Chronic/non-healing wound: Fails to progress normally through healing.\r\n    * Example: diabetic or pressure ulcer.\r\n* Surgical wounds may also be classified as clean, clean-contaminated, contaminated and dirty/infected.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Wound Healing\r\n\r\n* Definition: Wound healing is the biological process by which the body repairs damaged tissue and restores tissue continuity.\r\n* Phases:\r\n    1. Hemostasis – stops bleeding.\r\n    2. Inflammation – removes bacteria and debris.\r\n    3. Proliferation – granulation tissue, collagen, angiogenesis and epithelialization.\r\n    4. Remodeling – collagen reorganization and scar maturation. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Gangrene\r\n\r\n* Definition: Gangrene is tissue necrosis caused by inadequate blood supply, infection, or both.\r\n* Types:\r\n    * Dry gangrene: Usually due to ischemia.\r\n    * Wet gangrene: Necrotic tissue with infection.\r\n    * Gas gangrene: Rapidly progressive infection with gas formation. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n10. Cellulitis\r\n\r\n* Definition: Cellulitis is an acute bacterial infection of the skin and deeper subcutaneous tissues.\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Redness.\r\n    * Warmth.\r\n    * Swelling.\r\n    * Pain/tenderness.\r\n    * Fever may occur.\r\n* Treatment: Antibiotics, elevation and treatment of the underlying cause.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n11. Abscess\r\n\r\n* Definition: An abscess is a localized collection of pus within a tissue or body cavity, usually caused by infection.\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Pain.\r\n    * Swelling.\r\n    * Redness.\r\n    * Warmth.\r\n    * Fluctuant/pus-filled swelling.\r\n    * Fever may occur.\r\n* Treatment: Drainage when indicated + appropriate antibiotics.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n12. Amputation\r\n\r\n* Definition: Amputation is the surgical removal of part or all of a limb.\r\n* Indications: Severe trauma, gangrene, peripheral vascular disease, uncontrolled infection and malignancy.\r\n* Types: Above-knee, below-knee, above-elbow and below-elbow amputations.\r\n* Rehabilitation: Stump care, strengthening, ROM, prosthetic training and gait training.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n13. Phantom Limb\r\n\r\n* Definition: Phantom limb sensation is the feeling that an amputated limb is still present.\r\n* Phantom limb pain: Pain perceived in the missing limb.\r\n* Features: Burning, tingling, shooting pain or sensation of movement.\r\n* Management: Desensitization, mirror therapy, graded motor imagery, appropriate prosthetic rehabilitation and medical pain management.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n14. Varicose Veins\r\n\r\n* Definition: Varicose veins are dilated, elongated and tortuous superficial veins, usually of the lower limbs, caused mainly by venous valve incompetence.\r\n* Features: Visible twisted veins, aching, heaviness, ankle edema and skin changes.\r\n* Management: Exercise, leg elevation, compression when appropriate and selected surgical/endovenous treatment.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n15. Craniotomy\r\n\r\n* Definition: Craniotomy is a neurosurgical procedure in which a portion of the skull is temporarily removed to provide access to the brain and is usually replaced after surgery.\r\n* Indications: Brain tumour, aneurysm, hematoma, vascular lesions and other intracranial conditions.\r\n* Difference: In craniectomy, the bone flap is not immediately replaced.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n16. Laminectomy\r\n\r\n* Definition: Laminectomy is a spinal operation involving removal of part or all of the vertebral lamina to decompress the spinal cord or nerve roots.\r\n* Indications: Spinal stenosis, nerve-root compression, selected disc disease and spinal tumours.\r\n* Complications: Dural tear/CSF leak, infection, bleeding, neurological injury and spinal instability. 18. Postoperative Complications\r\n\r\n* Definition: Postoperative complications are unwanted conditions occurring after surgery that may delay recovery or cause morbidity.\r\n* Common complications:\r\n    * Shock.\r\n    * DVT.\r\n    * Wound infection.\r\n    * Atelectasis.\r\n    * Pneumonia.\r\n    * UTI.\r\n    * Pressure injuries.\r\n    * Hemorrhage.\r\n    * Wound dehiscence.\r\n    * Ileus.\r\n* Physiotherapy prevention: Early mobilization, breathing exercises, circulation exercises, ROM, positioning and functional rehabilitation.///////////////////\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-25T16:55:23+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
